>> title: No-Knead Bread
>> servings: 1
>> source: https://www.recipetineats.com/easy-yeast-bread-recipe-no-knead/#recipe

Mix @flour{450%g}, @yeast{2%tsp} and @salt{1.5%tsp} in a large bowl. Add @water{375%ml}, then use the handle of a wooden spoon to mix until all the flour is incorporated. Dough will be wet and sloppy – not kneadable, but not runny like cake batter. Adjust with more water or flour if needed for right consistency.

> If after 1 hour it doesn’t seem to be rising, move it somewhere warmer.

Cover with cling wrap or a plate, leave on counter for ~{2-3%hours} until it doubles in volume, it’s wobbly like jelly and the top is bubbly.

> At this stage, you can either bake immediately or refrigerate for up to 3 days for flavour development.

If you refrigerated dough, leave the bowl on the counter for ~{45-60%minutes} while the oven is preheating. Cold dough does not rise as well.

Preheat oven with a #dutch oven{26cm/10" or larger} in it to 450°F for ~{30%minutes} prior to baking.

Sprinkle work surface with @flour{1%tbsp}, scrape dough out of the bowl. Sprinkle top with @flour{0.5%tbsp}.

Using a #dough scraper{} or anything of similar shape, fold the sides inwards (about 6 folds) to roughly form a roundish shape. Don’t be too meticulous here – you’re about to deform it, it’s more about deflating the bubbles in the dough and forming a shape you can move.

Slide a large piece of #parchment/baking paper{} next to the dough, then flip the dough upside down onto the paper (ie seam side down, smooth side up). Slide/push it towards the middle, then reshape it into a round(ish) shape. Don't get too hung up about shape. In fact, lopsided = more ridges = more crunchy bits!

Remove piping hot dutch oven from oven. Use paper to place dough into pot, place lid on.

Bake for ~{30%minutes} covered, then ~{12%minutes} uncovered or until deep golden and crispy.

Cool on rack for ~{10%minutes} before slicing.